WN.lb(28,{"mt_h-z88yf9Pn":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Tom and Jas fall out once over a game and both shout. Is it bullying?","choices":["Yes, shouting is always bullying","No, it was once, and they are equals","Yes, if anyone heard it"],"answer":1,"hint":"Bullying is again and again, with more power.","explain":"A one-off fall-out between equals is not bullying. It is still worth sorting out."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"For {n} days, a group sends Sami mean messages in a game chat. What is the best word?","choices":["Peer pressure","Cyberbullying","A dilemma"],"answer":1,"hint":"Where is it happening, and is it again and again?","explain":"It is repeated, on purpose and online for {n} days, so it is cyberbullying.","params":{"n":[3,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"What is a bystander?","choices":["Someone who sees bullying and does nothing","Someone who stops bullying","Someone who starts bullying"],"answer":0,"hint":"A bystander just stands by.","explain":"A bystander sees it and does nothing. Doing nothing lets bullying carry on."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Ava finds £{n} on the floor. Nobody saw it drop. She hands it in. What does this show?","choices":["Peer pressure","Integrity","Consent"],"answer":1,"hint":"Which word means doing right when no one is watching?","explain":"Nobody would know if she kept the £{n}, so handing it in shows integrity.","params":{"n":[2,10]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Which is a responsibility, not a right?","choices":["To be safe","To learn","Not to stop others learning"],"answer":2,"hint":"A responsibility is something you should do for others.","explain":"Being safe and learning are rights. Not stopping others learning is the responsibility that protects their right."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Lena can tell the teacher who broke the window and upset her friend, or say nothing and feel bad. What is this?","choices":["A dilemma","Bullying","A right"],"answer":0,"hint":"Every choice here has a downside.","explain":"A dilemma is a hard choice where every option has a downside."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Before posting, Raj asks his {n} friends in the photo, and they all say yes. What did he get?","choices":["Peer pressure","Consent","A dilemma"],"answer":1,"hint":"He asked first and got a clear yes.","explain":"Asking all {n} friends and getting a clear yes is consent.","params":{"n":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Which upstander action is safest when you see bullying in the playground?","choices":["Fight the bully","Tell a teacher you trust","Laugh so they leave you alone"],"answer":1,"hint":"An upstander acts, and stays safe.","explain":"Telling a trusted adult is a safe way to act. You never have to fight, and laughing along is joining in."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"For {n} weeks, an older girl takes Ruby's snack and laughs. Ruby cannot make her stop. Which citizenship word names this: bullying, a fall-out or an accident?","choices":["A one-off fall-out","Bullying","An accident"],"answer":1,"hint":"Is it again and again, on purpose, and hard to stop?","explain":"It has gone on for {n} weeks, on purpose, and Ruby cannot stop it, so it is bullying.","params":{"n":[2,8]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"Kai sees Leo left out and teased. Kai goes and tells a teacher. What is Kai?","choices":["A bystander","An upstander","A bully"],"answer":1,"hint":"Did Kai do something safe to help?","explain":"Kai acted in a safe way by telling an adult, so he is an upstander. A bystander does nothing."},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"{n} friends tell Ella to share a photo of Mo. Mo has not said yes. What should Ella think about?","choices":["Whether she will get caught","Consent: Mo has not said yes","Whether the photo is funny"],"answer":1,"hint":"Which word means asking first and getting a clear yes?","explain":"Even with {n} friends pushing, the photo is Mo's to agree to. Without his yes, Ella should not share it.","params":{"n":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"You have the right to be safe at school. Which responsibility goes with it?","choices":["Not to hurt or scare others","To get the best seat","To win every game"],"answer":0,"hint":"Everyone else has the same right as you.","explain":"Others have the right to be safe too, so your responsibility is not to hurt or scare them."},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"Which post shows good digital citizenship?","choices":["A photo of a friend, after she said yes","A joke about a classmate's hair","Your school name and street"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is it safe, kind and fair?","explain":"Asking first is fair, and the post is kind and safe. The joke is unkind, and the address is not safe."}],"explain":{"kid":["Bullying is hurting someone again and again, on purpose. It is hard for them to make it stop. A fall-out between two friends, just once, is not bullying. An accident is not bullying. It is still worth fixing. Bullying online, on phones or games, is called cyberbullying.","A bystander sees bullying and does nothing. An upstander sees it and does something safe. You can tell a teacher or ask the child to play. You never have to fight. Telling an adult is not telling tales. It is how bullying stops.","Peer pressure is when friends push you to do something. A good reason helps you say no. Consent means you ask first and get a clear yes. A no means no. Integrity means you do the right thing even when no one sees. \"Nobody will find out\" is not a reason. It is just about getting caught.","A right is something every person should have, like being safe. A responsibility is something you should do, so others keep their rights too. Ethical means right and fair. A dilemma is a hard choice where every choice has a bad side. Digital citizenship means being safe, kind and fair online."],"adult":["This topic gives children aged 7 to 11 the words for right and wrong with other people: bullying, cyberbullying, bystander, upstander, peer pressure, consent, integrity, rights, responsibilities, ethical, dilemma and digital citizenship. Naming a situation helps a child decide what to do and ask for help.","The lab uses five short scenes. Learners set three switches to test whether a story is bullying. They move themselves to a choice in a playground. They pull a peer-pressure rope back with real reasons. They balance rights with responsibilities and fix a post before it goes online.","Common mistakes: calling every mean moment bullying, when bullying is repeated, deliberate and involves a power imbalance. Many children think a bystander is not involved, or that an upstander must confront the bully, when telling a trusted adult counts. Some treat a right as permission to do anything, or think a photo is fine to share without asking.","At home, use the words about real events, books and shows. Ask what an upstander could do here, or whether someone said yes first. Agree together what your child will do if they see bullying online: save it, block, and tell you."]},"familyTip":"Pick a story from a book or TV show and ask: who was a bystander, who was an upstander, and was there a dilemma? Then agree one safe thing your child would do in that spot.","keyIdea":"Bullying is hurting someone again and again, on purpose, when it is hard for them to stop it; online it is cyberbullying. A bystander sees it and does nothing, and an upstander acts in a safe way. Peer pressure is feeling pushed by friends. Consent means asking and getting a clear yes. Integrity is doing right when no one is watching. Every right comes with a responsibility. Ethical means right and fair, and a dilemma is a hard choice where every option has a downside. Digital citizenship is being safe, kind and fair online.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Bystander or upstander? Ask: did they do something safe to help?","opts":{"bins":["Upstander","Bystander"],"items":[["Tells a teacher",0],["Asks the child to play",0],["Reports a mean post",0],["Walks past",1],["Looks away and says nothing",1],["Scrolls past a mean post",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagcit","caption":"Flagship lab: test stories with bullying switches, choose bystander or upstander, pull back peer pressure, balance rights and fix a post.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_sRM9-k3NOq":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"What is a balanced budget?","choices":["Income minus spending minus saving equals zero, so every pound has a job","Spending is lower than last month","You never buy any wants"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about every pound having a planned use.","explain":"A balanced budget plans every pound: spending plus saving equals income, so nothing is left without a job."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m2","prompt":"A £{p} snack every school day for 190 days. How much is that over the year, in pounds?","answer":"p*190","hint":"Multiply the cost by the number of days.","explain":"£{p} × 190 = £{=p*190} a year.","params":{"p":[1,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Why does paying yourself first work better than saving what is left?","choices":["Money left in your account tends to get spent","Banks charge you less","It earns double interest"],"answer":0,"hint":"What happens to money that sits in your current account?","explain":"Money in a current account tends to get spent. Moving savings on payday takes the choice away."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m4","prompt":"Essential costs are £{e}00 a month. About how much should an emergency fund of three months hold, in pounds?","answer":"e*300","hint":"Multiply one month of essentials by three.","explain":"An emergency fund covers about three months of essential costs. 3 × £{e}00 = £{=e*300}, so a broken boiler or a lost shift does not push you into debt.","params":{"e":[6,12]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Which is a want rather than a need?","choices":["Takeaway on Friday nights","Food shopping","Bus fare to work"],"answer":0,"hint":"Could you live and get to work without it?","explain":"A takeaway is a want; food shopping and bus fare to work are needs."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Jade saves £50 a month for 2 years at 4% AER. Why is her interest about £46, not £96?","choices":["Each £50 only earns from the day it goes in","The bank keeps half","Interest is only paid in the first year"],"answer":0,"hint":"Does the last payment earn for two years?","explain":"Early payments earn for nearly two years, later ones for only a few months, so the interest is about £46."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Your phone screen breaks and the repair is £120. Which plan protects you best?","choices":["An emergency fund you set up in advance","Pay on a credit card and worry later","Skip rent this month"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which choice avoids borrowing and missing a need?","explain":"An emergency fund covers the surprise without debt or missing a need."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"A budget shows £60 with no job and savings of £0. What is the best fix?","choices":["Give the £60 a job, such as saving","Leave it for the weekend","Spend it now before it disappears"],"answer":0,"hint":"Every pound should have a planned use.","explain":"Give it a job. Planning the £60 as saving makes the budget balance and builds a buffer."},{"type":"mc","id":"m9","prompt":"Mia moves £{n}0 to savings on payday every month. After a year she has saved…","choices":["£{=n*120}, before any interest","£{n}0","Nothing, because she spent it"],"answer":0,"hint":"Twelve months of the same payment.","explain":"£{n}0 × 12 = £{=n*120}, plus any interest.","params":{"n":[2,6]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Which of these is a need for someone who works full time?","choices":["Rent for their room","A second streaming service","New trainers when the old pair is fine"],"answer":0,"hint":"A need keeps you housed, fed, safe or able to work.","explain":"Rent is a need because it keeps a roof over your head. The others are wants."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A drink costs £{p} and you buy one every day for a 30-day month. How much do you spend in pounds?","answer":"p*30","hint":"Multiply the daily cost by the number of days.","explain":"£{p} × 30 = £{=p*30} for the month.","params":{"p":[2,6]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"Take-home pay is £1,000. Rent is £450, food is £{f}0 and travel is £100. How much is left for fun and saving, in pounds?","answer":"1000-450-f*10-100","hint":"Take each cost away from the take-home pay.","explain":"£1,000 − £450 − £{f}0 − £100 = £{=1000-450-f*10-100}.","params":{"f":[12,20]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Leo wants to save £{n}0 a month. He saves whatever is left at the end of each month and usually saves almost nothing. What is the best change?","choices":["Move £{n}0 to savings on payday by standing order","Wait for a month when he spends less","Borrow on a card when he runs short"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which option happens before the money can be spent?","explain":"Paying yourself first moves £{n}0 out on payday, so it cannot drift into spending. Borrowing makes next month harder.","params":{"n":[3,8]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"£200 sits in a savings account at 5% AER for one year. How much interest does it earn?","choices":["£10","£100","£205"],"answer":0,"hint":"5% means 5 pence for every pound.","explain":"5% of £200 is £10. The total after one year is £210."}],"explain":{"kid":["A budget is a plan for your money before you spend it. Start with needs: things that keep you housed, fed, safe and able to work. Then plan saving. Wants come out of what is left.","Small costs add up. A £4.50 drink every day is £135 a month and over £1,600 a year. To see a small cost clearly, times it by how often you pay it.","Pay yourself first. Money left in your account tends to get spent, so move your saving on payday, before you spend. A standing order makes it happen without you thinking.","Build an emergency fund. Aim for about three months of your essential costs. Then a broken phone or a big bill does not push you into debt. Savings accounts pay interest, so the money grows the longer you leave it."],"adult":["This topic, for ages 14 to 16, covers the core of a personal budget: income, needs, wants and saving, all planned before the money is spent. A balanced budget gives every pound a job, so income minus spending minus saving is zero. Learners use take-home pay, not salary, because tax and National Insurance come out first.","Behavioural research explains why pay yourself first works. People treat money in a current account as money to spend, and saving what is left often leaves very little. Moving savings on payday by standing order removes the choice. Automatic schemes like workplace pension enrolment raise saving for the same reason.","Common mistakes: treating every cost as a need, ignoring small repeated costs, and thinking interest is simply the rate times all the money paid in. With £50 a month for two years at 4% AER, the interest is about £46, not £96, because each payment only earns from the day it arrives. MoneyHelper suggests an emergency fund of about three months of essential outgoings.","In the lab, learners sort costs into needs and wants and balance a £1,200 monthly budget for a 19-year-old apprentice. They tap a month of £4.50 drinks and run six months of saving what is left against paying yourself first. Then they grow £50 a month at 4% AER, switching to 0% to compare."]},"familyTip":"Show your teenager one real monthly bill and one bank statement (or a made-up one). Together, mark each line as a need, a want or saving, then add up the small repeated costs for a month.","keyIdea":"A budget gives every pound a job before the month starts: needs first, then saving, then wants. Small regular costs add up to big yearly ones. Saving works best when it comes out first, on payday, and an emergency fund of about three months of essential costs stops one bad surprise turning into debt. Interest pays you for leaving money in, and it builds over time.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Fixed cost, flexible cost or saving? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Fixed cost","Flexible cost","Saving"],"items":[["Rent",0],["Phone contract",0],["Food shopping",1],["Takeaways",1],["Emergency fund",2],["Payday standing order to savings",2]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagbudget","caption":"Flagship lab: sort needs and wants, balance a £1,200 budget, see small costs add up, pay yourself first and watch interest grow.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_VD2nIGUEI-":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Your ball rolls into the road. What do you do?","choices":["Stop and tell a grown-up","Run and get it fast","Wait in the road for it"],"answer":0,"hint":"Cars are fast. Who should get the ball?","explain":"Stop and tell a grown-up. Cars are fast and may not see you, so a grown-up gets the ball."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Which one is hot and can burn you?","choices":["A lit candle","A teddy","An apple"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which one has a flame that can burn?","explain":"A lit candle is hot and can burn. A teddy and an apple are OK to touch."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Ben says, \"I only hold hands by the road when Dad is looking.\" Is that right?","choices":["No, cars are fast every time","Yes, rules are just for Dad","Yes, if the road is small"],"answer":0,"hint":"Are cars slower when Dad is not looking?","explain":"Ben is not right. The rule keeps him safe from cars every time, whoever is watching."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Dad has cooked {n} pancakes. The pan is on the cooker. What do you do?","choices":["Look from far away","Touch the pan","Climb up to see"],"answer":0,"hint":"A pan on the cooker can be very hot.","explain":"Look from far away. A pan on the cooker can burn you, even when you cannot see a flame.","params":{"n":[3,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Why do we hold hands by the road?","choices":["Cars are big and fast","So we can go faster","Because it is fun"],"answer":0,"hint":"What is on the road that could hurt us?","explain":"We hold hands because cars are big and fast. A grown-up can keep you on the path."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"It is {n} minutes to snack time. You are inside. How do you get to the table?","choices":["Walk","Run as fast as you can","Slide on the floor"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which way stops you tripping and bumping?","explain":"Walk. Running or sliding inside can make you trip and bump, even when you are in a hurry.","params":{"n":[2,10]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Why do we stay with our grown-up at the park?","choices":["So we do not get lost","So they can carry us","Because parks are boring"],"answer":0,"hint":"What could happen if you wander away?","explain":"We stay close so we do not get lost. Our grown-up can see us and help if we need it."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Which rule keeps us safe by the road?","choices":["Stop, look and listen","Run across quickly","Close your eyes"],"answer":0,"hint":"What do we do at the kerb before we cross?","explain":"Stop, look and listen at the kerb, holding a grown-up's hand, before crossing."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"You are by a busy road with your grown-up. What do you do?","choices":["Hold their hand","Run ahead","Walk on your own"],"answer":0,"hint":"Cars are big and fast. Who keeps you safe by the road?","explain":"Hold your grown-up's hand. Cars are big and fast, and drivers might not see a small child."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"There are {n} toys on the playroom floor. Kit wants to get to the door. What is safest?","choices":["Walk and step round the toys","Run fast to the door","Jump over the toys"],"answer":0,"hint":"What can happen if you run past toys on the floor?","explain":"Walking is safest. If Kit runs, Kit can trip on the toys and bump. Walking gives time to see them.","params":{"n":[2,7]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"The kettle clicked off a minute ago. There is no steam now. Is it safe to touch?","choices":["No, it can still be hot","Yes, no steam means cold","Yes, if you are quick"],"answer":0,"hint":"Can you always see when something is hot?","explain":"No. A kettle stays hot for a long time after the steam stops. Keep away and let a grown-up use it."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Mia and her mum are in a big shop with {n} aisles. Mia sees the toys. What should she do?","choices":["Stay with her mum","Go to the toys on her own","Hide behind a shelf"],"answer":0,"hint":"What could happen in a big, busy shop if Mia goes off alone?","explain":"Mia should stay with her mum. In a big shop she could get lost. She can ask Mum to come and look at the toys with her.","params":{"n":[4,12]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"Why do we walk, not run, inside?","choices":["So we do not trip and bump","Because running is bad","So we get there first"],"answer":0,"hint":"What can happen when you run in a room full of things?","explain":"We walk inside so we do not trip on things or bump into people and furniture."}],"explain":{"kid":["By the road, hold hands with your grown-up. Cars are big and fast.","Inside, we walk. If we run, we can trip on toys and bump our heads.","Hot things can burn. A kettle can be hot with no steam. Keep away.","In the shop, stay with your grown-up. Then you will not get lost. Every rule has a reason."],"adult":["This topic, for ages 3 to 4, is about a few simple safety rules: hold hands near roads, walk indoors, keep away from hot things and stay close in busy places. Your child also learns to say why each rule matters. It leads on to everyday safety awareness and to safe choices made without a reminder.","Children of this age keep a rule much better when they know its reason. \"Hold my hand\" works better as \"Hold my hand, because cars are big and fast and drivers might not see you.\" Short reasons that name the danger (cars, tripping, burns, getting lost) work best. Practise the rule in the real place. At the kerb, stop, look and listen before every crossing, even on quiet roads.","Common mistakes: thinking a kettle, iron or pan is safe once the steam or flame has gone (they stay hot for a long time), running after a ball into the road, thinking a rule only counts when a grown-up is watching, and seeing rules as a punishment, not protection. Young children also cannot judge how fast cars are going, so they need a hand to hold, not just a warning.","In the lab your child holds hands and waits for two cars to pass before crossing. Kit trips when running indoors and gets to the door safely when walking. Then your child sorts a teapot and a candle from a teddy and an apple, matches each rule to its reason, and picks the safe choice in three quick moments."]},"familyTip":"On your next walk, let your child be the \"safety boss\" at each kerb: they tell you to stop, look and listen, and say why. At home, point to the kettle after it clicks off and ask, \"Hot or OK to touch?\"","keyIdea":"Some rules are there to stop us getting hurt. Hold a grown-up's hand by the road, because cars are big and fast. Walk, do not run, inside, so we do not trip and bump. Keep away from hot things like cookers, kettles and candles, because they can burn, even when you cannot see steam or fire. Stay with your grown-up in busy places so you do not get lost. Knowing why a rule matters makes it easier to keep.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Safe or not safe? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Safe","Not safe"],"items":[["hold hands by the road",0],["walk inside",0],["stay with a grown-up",0],["run into the road",1],["touch the cooker",1],["run off in the shop",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagsafe","caption":"Flagship lab: hold hands and wait for the cars, walk past the toys, sort hot things, match each rule to its reason, then pick the safe choice.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_uTKgmWqSoI":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"What is a consequence?","choices":["What happens because of a choice","A kind of game","Only a punishment"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think: choice, then what happens next.","explain":"A consequence is what happens because of a choice. It can be good, like a happy friend, or bad, like a fall."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Why do we have a rule to hold hands by the road?","choices":["To keep us safe from cars","To stop us having fun","Because grown-ups are bored"],"answer":0,"hint":"What could happen near a busy road?","explain":"The rule keeps us safe. Cars are fast, and holding hands stops you running into the road."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"A friendly man you do not know says: come and see my puppy. What do you do?","choices":["Say no and tell a trusted adult","Go with him","Keep it a secret"],"answer":0,"hint":"Do you know him? Is he a trusted adult?","explain":"Say no and tell a trusted adult. Friendly is not the same as trusted. Never go with a stranger."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Maya feeds her {n} fish every morning without being asked. What is she?","choices":["Responsible","Unfair","Unsafe"],"answer":0,"hint":"She does her job and looks after her pets.","explain":"Maya is responsible. She looks after all {n} fish and does her job every day.","params":{"n":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"You leave your coat at the park. It is {n} degrees and you are cold. What is being cold?","choices":["A consequence","A rule","Being honest"],"answer":0,"hint":"It happened because of what you did.","explain":"Being cold is a consequence. It happened because you left your coat behind, and {n} degrees is chilly.","params":{"n":[3,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"You tell the truth about a mistake. What usually happens?","choices":["People trust you and help fix it","You always get in big trouble","Nothing ever happens"],"answer":0,"hint":"Honest words help people trust you.","explain":"People trust you and help you fix it. Being honest is right, even when it feels hard."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Which is a safe choice?","choices":["Wear a helmet on my bike","Ride in the road with no helmet","Climb on the roof"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which one stops you getting hurt?","explain":"Wearing a helmet is safe. It protects your head if you fall. The others could hurt you."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"You share your {n} stickers with a friend. Your friend smiles. What is the smile?","choices":["A consequence","A rule","A secret"],"answer":0,"hint":"It happened because of your choice.","explain":"The smile is a consequence. It happened because you chose to share your {n} stickers. Good choices have consequences too.","params":{"n":[2,8]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"You broke a cup. Which words are honest?","choices":["I broke it. I am sorry.","The dog did it.","It was broken before."],"answer":0,"hint":"Honest means telling what really happened.","explain":"\"I broke it. I am sorry\" is honest. Blaming the dog or saying it was broken before is not true."},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"{n} children want the swing. They each get a turn. What word is that?","choices":["Fair","Unsafe","Honest"],"answer":0,"hint":"Everyone gets the same go.","explain":"It is fair. All {n} children get a turn, with no favourites.","params":{"n":[3,7]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Who is a trusted adult?","choices":["My teacher","A stranger in the park","Someone online I do not know"],"answer":0,"hint":"A trusted adult is someone you know, who keeps you safe.","explain":"Your teacher is a trusted adult. You know them and they help keep you safe. A stranger is not, even if they seem kind."}],"explain":{"kid":["A choice is when you pick what to do. Keep the ball, or give it back? A consequence is what happens next. It happens because of your choice. Good choices have consequences too. You give the ball back. Your friend is happy. You grab the cookie. Your brother is cross.","Honest means you tell the truth. Say I broke it, not the cat did it. Then you can fix it with help.","A rule tells us what to do. We hold hands by the road, so we are safe. Fair means everyone gets a turn. Responsible means you do your jobs, like feeding your pet.","Feel unsafe? Tell a trusted adult. That is a grown-up you know, like your teacher, your mum or your grandma. A stranger is not one, even a kind one."],"adult":["This topic gives five to eight year olds the words for making decisions: choice, consequence, rule, safe, fair, honest, responsible, right and wrong, and trusted adult. Naming the ideas helps children stop and think before they act, and gives them words to talk a choice through with you.","The key link is between a choice and its consequence. Children often think consequence means punishment. Show them that good choices have consequences too, such as a happy friend or easier reading. Ask: what might happen next if you do that?","Common mistakes: thinking only bad choices have consequences; thinking a rule is just to stop fun, not to keep people safe and fair; and thinking telling the truth always gets you in trouble. A key safety slip is thinking that a friendly person is a trusted adult. A trusted adult is someone the child knows who helps keep them safe, such as a parent, a carer, a teacher or a police officer. They should never go with a stranger, however kind.","In the lab your child walks down both roads of a choice to see each consequence, sorts consequences into good and bad, and picks the honest words after a mistake. Then they bring the trusted adults into a safe circle and match the words fair, honest, rule, safe and responsible to pictures."]},"familyTip":"At bedtime, play 'What happens next?': describe a small choice from the day (sharing a toy, leaving a coat at school) and ask what the consequence was. Together, make a list of your child's trusted adults and say their names out loud.","keyIdea":"A choice is when you pick what to do. A consequence is what happens because of that choice, and it can be good or bad. A rule tells everyone what to do so we stay safe and are fair. Honest means telling the truth, even after a mistake. Responsible means doing your jobs and looking after things. A trusted adult is a grown-up you know who helps keep you safe; if something feels unsafe, tell one.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Is it a safe choice or an unsafe choice? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["🛡️ Safe","⚠️ Unsafe"],"items":[["Wear a helmet on my bike",0],["Run across the road",1],["Tell my teacher I feel scared",0],["Go off with a stranger",1],["Hold hands by the road",0],["Climb on the cooker",1],["Ask before I touch medicine",0],["Keep a secret that scares me",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagdecide","caption":"Flagship lab: walk down both roads of a choice, sort consequences, pick honest words, bring trusted adults into the circle and name each picture.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_XQPZwsjr3_":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"What can Pip the dog eat?","choices":["Her own dog food","Chocolate","Your sweets"],"answer":0,"hint":"Some of our treats make dogs ill.","explain":"Dogs eat dog food. Chocolate and sweets can make a dog very poorly."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Dad has lots of heavy shopping. What do you say?","choices":["Can I help?","Not my job","I want a biscuit"],"answer":0,"hint":"Good helpers ask before they jump in.","explain":"\"Can I help?\" is kind. Dad can give you a small bag to carry."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m3","prompt":"There are {n} blocks on the floor. Mia puts {k} in the box. How many are still on the floor?","answer":"n-k","hint":"Take away the ones Mia put in the box.","explain":"{n} take away {k} leaves {=n-k} blocks on the floor. Keep tidying!","params":{"n":[4,5],"k":[1,3]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Which job is right for you?","choices":["Washing the carrots","Chopping with a sharp knife","Lifting the hot pan"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which one can not cut or burn you?","explain":"Washing carrots is safe. Sharp knives and hot pans are grown-up jobs."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Your friend spills juice. What do you do?","choices":["Get a cloth and help","Laugh at them","Walk away"],"answer":0,"hint":"What would make your friend feel better?","explain":"Helping wipe it up is kind and makes the mess go away fast."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"You push all the toys under the sofa. Is that tidy?","choices":["No, they need to go home","Yes, it looks tidy","Yes, toys like it there"],"answer":0,"hint":"Can you find them next time?","explain":"Hidden is not tidy. Toys go back to their own home so we can find them."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"Four children each have one cup. {n} more friends come. How many cups now?","answer":"4+n","hint":"Start at 4 cups and count on one for each new friend.","explain":"4 and {n} more makes {=4+n} cups, one for each child.","params":{"n":[1,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Who can help tidy up?","choices":["Everyone, me too","Only grown-ups","Only big kids"],"answer":0,"hint":"Can small hands lift a toy?","explain":"Everyone can help, even little ones. When we all help, it is done fast."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Tidy up time! Where does a book go?","choices":["On the shelf","Under the sofa","On the floor"],"answer":0,"hint":"Where can you find it next time?","explain":"Books go back on the shelf. Then you can find them, and no one steps on them."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"There are {n} toys on the floor. You put them all in the toy box. How many toys are in the box?","answer":"n","hint":"Count each toy as you put it in.","explain":"You put in every toy, so {n} toys are in the box. The floor is clear.","params":{"n":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Why do we tidy toys off the floor?","choices":["So no one trips","So the toys get lost","So we never play again"],"answer":0,"hint":"What can happen if you step on a toy?","explain":"Toys on the floor can make someone trip and fall. Tidying keeps everyone safe."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"{n} people sit down for snack. You give one cup to each person. How many cups do you need?","answer":"n","hint":"One cup for each person. Count the people.","explain":"{n} people need {n} cups, one each.","params":{"n":[2,5]}}],"explain":{"kid":["Every toy has a home. Blocks go in the toy box. Books go on the shelf. Coats go on the peg. When we put things back, we can find them next time.","Toys on the floor can trip us. Ouch! A tidy floor keeps everyone safe. Pushing toys under the sofa is not tidy. Put them in their home.","You can help at home. Set out one cup for each person. Give the dog her own dinner and some water. Wash the carrots with Gran.","Ask, \"Can I help?\" Pick a small job. Sharp knives, hot pans and heavy bags are for grown-ups. When we all help, the job is quick and everyone is happy."],"adult":["Development Matters (2023) covers this in Personal, Social and Emotional Development. At 3 and 4, children develop their sense of responsibility and membership of a community. They also become more independent in meeting their own care needs. Small jobs lead on to the Managing Self early learning goal at the end of Reception.","Children tidy best when each thing has one clear home, with a picture label on the box or shelf. Say the reason out loud: so we can find it, so no one trips. Setting a cup for each person is early maths too. It uses one-to-one correspondence. Real jobs done next to an adult build more pride than praise alone.","Common mistakes: redoing the job straight away, so the child learns their help is not wanted. Letting 'tidy' mean hiding things. Offering jobs that are too big, sharp or hot. Be careful with pets. Chocolate, grapes and raisins are poisonous to dogs. Children give only the pet's own dinner, and an adult measures it.","In the lab your child puts six things in their homes, and finds that under the sofa is refused. They clear four toys so Teddy can walk to the door without tripping. They set one cup for each of four people. They give Pip her dinner and water but not chocolate. Then they pick kind jobs of the right size in three helping scenes."]},"familyTip":"Give each kind of toy one home with a picture on the box. At tidy-up time, say the reason (\"so no one trips\") and work alongside your child for the first few minutes, then let them finish one small job alone and leave it as they did it.","keyIdea":"Helping means doing small jobs that make things better for everyone: putting toys back in their own home, setting out a cup for each person, giving a pet its own dinner and water. We tidy so things can be found again and so no one trips. Good helpers ask \"Can I help?\" and pick a job that is the right size, leaving sharp, hot and heavy jobs to grown-ups.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"A small job for me, or a job for a grown-up? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["🙋 I can help","🧑 Grown-up job"],"items":[["putting toys in the box",0],["setting out the cups",0],["watering a plant",0],["feeding the fish with a grown-up",0],["chopping with a sharp knife",1],["lifting a hot pan",1],["carrying a heavy box",1],["plugging in the kettle",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagtidy","caption":"Flagship lab: put things in their homes, clear the path so no one trips, set a cup for each person, feed the dog safely and pick a right-sized helping job.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_3sQ7ndzhV0":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"What is compound interest?","choices":["Interest charged on the principal and on earlier interest","Interest charged only on the amount first borrowed","A fee for paying late"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of the £1,000 that grew to about £1,950 in three years.","explain":"Compound interest is charged on the principal and on interest already added, so the debt grows faster each year."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Why does paying only the minimum on a credit card take so long?","choices":["The minimum shrinks as the balance shrinks, and most of it pays interest","The bank adds a new fee every month","The APR goes up each year"],"answer":0,"hint":"The minimum is the interest plus 1% of the balance.","explain":"Most of each minimum payment covers interest, and the minimum falls as the balance falls, so £1,500 takes over 22 years."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m3","prompt":"£{p}00 is borrowed at 10% a year, compounded yearly, with nothing repaid. What is owed after 2 years, in pounds?","answer":"p*121","hint":"Multiply by 1.1 for each year: 1.1 × 1.1 = 1.21.","explain":"£{p}00 × 1.21 = £{=p*121}. The second year charges interest on the first year's interest too.","params":{"p":[2,9]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m4","prompt":"You borrow £1,000 and repay {n} monthly payments of £{m}. What is the cost of credit, in pounds?","answer":"n*m-1000","hint":"Total repaid minus the amount borrowed.","explain":"{n} × £{m} = £{=n*m}. Take away the £1,000 borrowed: the cost of credit is £{=n*m-1000}.","params":{"n":[12,24],"m":[90,120]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Which is the best way to compare two loans?","choices":["Compare the APR and the total amount repayable","Pick the lowest monthly payment","Pick the longest term"],"answer":0,"hint":"Remember Loan A and Loan B in the lab.","explain":"The APR and the total repayable show the real cost. A lower monthly payment over a longer term usually costs more."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"What can happen if you miss a buy now, pay later payment?","choices":["Late fees and a mark on your credit file","Nothing, because it is not real credit","The shop takes the item back and the debt ends"],"answer":0,"hint":"Most of these deals are now regulated credit.","explain":"Missed payments can bring late fees and can be reported to credit reference agencies, which affects future borrowing."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Two graduates both earn £35,000. One owes £45,000 on Plan 5 and one owes £60,000. Who repays more each month?","choices":["They repay the same","The one who owes £60,000","The one who owes £45,000"],"answer":0,"hint":"What decides the Plan 5 repayment?","explain":"Plan 5 repayments are 9% of earnings above £25,000, so both pay £900 a year, about £75 a month."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"What is a credit file?","choices":["A record of how you have used credit, checked by lenders","A list of your savings","A bank's list of interest rates"],"answer":0,"hint":"Lenders look at it before they lend to you.","explain":"A credit file records your borrowing and repayments. Lenders check it for phones, car finance and mortgages."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"What does APR stand for, and what does it show?","choices":["Annual percentage rate: the yearly cost of borrowing, including fees","Annual payment rate: the amount you repay each month","Average principal remaining: what you still owe"],"answer":0,"hint":"It is a percentage that lets you compare the cost of different deals.","explain":"APR is the annual percentage rate. It shows the yearly cost of borrowing, including interest and most fees."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"On a Plan 5 student loan you repay 9% of earnings above £25,000. How much do you repay in a year on a salary of £{s},000?","answer":"(s-25)*90","hint":"Find how much of the salary is above £25,000, then take 9% of it.","explain":"£{s},000 − £25,000 = £{=(s-25)*1000}. 9% of that is £{=(s-25)*90} a year.","params":{"s":[27,45]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"You make {n} buys of £60 each, all pay in 3: a third now, then a third in each of the next two months. How much is due next month, in pounds?","answer":"n*20","hint":"Each £60 buy needs £20 next month.","explain":"{n} × £20 = £{=n*20} due next month, and the same the month after.","params":{"n":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Loan A is £99.92 a month for 36 months. Loan B is £78.73 a month for 84 months. Which costs less in total?","choices":["Loan A","Loan B","They cost the same"],"answer":0,"hint":"Multiply each monthly payment by the number of months.","explain":"Loan A totals about £3,597 and Loan B about £6,613. The lower monthly payment costs £3,016 more."}],"explain":{"kid":["Credit is borrowed money. A credit card, an overdraft, a loan and buy now, pay later are all credit. The amount you borrow is the principal. Interest is the extra you pay for borrowing it. Debt is what you still owe.","The APR is the yearly cost of borrowing as a percentage. It includes interest and most fees, so it is the fair way to compare deals. A lower APR is cheaper. Interest also compounds. Each month you pay interest on last month's interest too. Leave £1,000 on a card at 24.9% APR for three years and you owe about £1,950.","A card only asks for a small minimum payment. On £1,500 at 24.9%, the minimum takes over 22 years and costs about £2,600 in interest. Paying a fixed £100 a month clears it in 18 months for £277. With loans, look at the total you repay, not just the monthly payment. A longer term means more months of interest.","Missed payments go on your credit file. Lenders check it when you want a phone contract, a car loan or a mortgage. A Plan 5 student loan is different. You pay 9% of what you earn above £25,000. If you earn less, you pay nothing. After 40 years, what is left is written off."],"adult":["This lesson covers the money parts of PSHE Living in the Wider World at Key Stage 5. It builds on financial education in KS3 and KS4 citizenship. Pupils should use the terms credit, debt, principal, interest, APR, compound interest, minimum repayment, overdraft, credit file and deferred payment credit. They should also judge the total cost of credit.","All figures in the lab are worked out from the stated rates. A 24.9% APR is a monthly rate of about 1.87%. The minimum payment is modelled as interest plus 1% of the balance, or £5 if higher, which is the usual rule for UK cards. The loan figures use standard repayment maths. Most buy now, pay later deals became regulated by the FCA in July 2026, with checks on whether people can afford them.","Common mistakes: comparing loans on the monthly payment instead of the total repayable; thinking the minimum payment is a sensible plan; treating buy now, pay later as not being real debt; and ignoring overdraft charges. Many students also misread student loans. On Plan 5, what you owe does not change what you pay each month. It is 9% of earnings above £25,000, and interest is set by the RPI.","In the lab, your teenager drags a slider through three years of compound interest and tries five ways to clear £1,500. They reveal the full cost of two £3,000 loans and pick the cheaper one. Then they stack four pay in 3 buys against a £50 budget and slide a salary to see Plan 5 repayments."]},"familyTip":"Next time a loan, phone contract or pay in 3 offer comes up, ask your teenager to find two numbers: the APR and the total amount repayable. Then ask what happens if one payment is missed.","keyIdea":"Credit means borrowing money now and paying it back later, usually with interest. The APR shows the yearly cost of borrowing, including fees, so it lets you compare deals. Interest compounds: you pay interest on earlier interest. Paying only the minimum on a credit card can take decades, a lower monthly payment over a longer term often costs more in total, and small buy now, pay later instalments add up. A Plan 5 student loan works differently: you repay 9% of earnings above £25,000, whatever you owe.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Costly or careful? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["💸 Costly: adds interest, fees or risk","✅ Careful: keeps the cost of credit down"],"items":[["Paying only the minimum on a card",0],["Choosing a loan on the lowest monthly payment",0],["Stacking several pay in 3 buys",0],["Going over your agreed overdraft",0],["Setting a fixed payment above the minimum",1],["Comparing deals by APR and total repayable",1],["Paying the card in full each month",1],["Checking your credit file for errors",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagdebt","caption":"Flagship lab: watch interest compound, beat the minimum-payment trap, compare loans by total cost, stack pay in 3 buys and slide a Plan 5 salary.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_Wc6cOTQ1bA":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"You find a teddy on a bench in the park. No one is there. What should you do?","choices":["Give it to a grown-up","Keep it, finders keepers","Throw it away"],"answer":0,"hint":"Does the teddy still belong to someone?","explain":"The teddy still belongs to someone. A grown-up can help find the owner."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Leo broke a cup by accident and told his mum. How does his mum feel about him telling her?","choices":["Glad he told the truth","Cross that he told her","She does not care"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about trust. Is it good to know the truth?","explain":"Mum is glad Leo told the truth. She can trust him, and they can fix it together."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Ben is sitting on his own at playtime and looks sad. What is a kind choice?","choices":["Ask Ben to play","Walk past Ben","Point at Ben and laugh"],"answer":0,"hint":"How would you feel if you were on your own?","explain":"Asking Ben to play helps him feel wanted. Walking past or laughing makes him feel worse."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Your friend says: let's take a sweet from the shop, no one will see. What do you do?","choices":["Say no","Take one","Take one for your friend"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is it right to take something without paying, even if no one sees?","explain":"Taking without paying is stealing, even when no one sees. The right thing is to say no."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"There are {=3*k} stickers for 3 friends. To be fair, how many stickers does each friend get?","answer":"k","hint":"Share them out one at a time: one for each friend, again and again.","explain":"{=3*k} stickers shared the same between 3 friends is {k} each.","params":{"k":[1,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Jo broke a toy. She says her little brother did it. Why is that wrong?","choices":["It is a lie and her brother gets told off","It is fine if no one finds out","Little brothers break things"],"answer":0,"hint":"Who gets in trouble, and is it true?","explain":"Blaming her brother is a lie, and he gets told off for something he did not do."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"Zara has {a} toy cars and Tom has none. How many should Zara give Tom so they each have {=a/2}?","answer":"a/2","hint":"Fair means the same. Give Tom one car at a time until both have the same.","explain":"Zara gives Tom {=a/2} cars. Then Zara has {=a/2} and Tom has {=a/2}, so it is fair.","params":{"a":[2,8]},"where":"a%2==0"},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Which one is a right choice?","choices":["Saying sorry when you hurt someone","Grabbing the best toy first","Telling a friend's secret"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which one is honest, fair or kind?","explain":"Saying sorry is honest and kind. Grabbing and telling secrets are unfair and unkind."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Mia spills juice on the sofa by accident. What is the right thing to do?","choices":["Tell a grown-up","Put a cushion over it","Say the dog did it"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which choice is honest and helps fix the spill?","explain":"Telling a grown-up is honest, and they can help clean it. Hiding it or blaming the dog is not honest."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"There are {a} children. To be fair, each child gets {b} grapes. How many grapes do we need?","answer":"a*b","hint":"Count {b} grapes for each child, then add them all up.","explain":"{a} children with {b} grapes each need {=a*b} grapes. Then each child has the same.","params":{"a":[2,4],"b":[1,3]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Sam wants to use Ava's new pens. What should he do first?","choices":["Ask Ava","Take them while she is out","Take just one"],"answer":0,"hint":"The pens belong to Ava. What do we do with things that are not ours?","explain":"The pens are Ava's, so Sam asks first. Taking even one without asking is not right."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"There are {=2*k} biscuits for 2 children. To be fair, how many biscuits does each child get?","answer":"k","hint":"Give one to each child, then one more to each, until the biscuits are all gone.","explain":"{=2*k} biscuits shared the same between 2 children is {k} each.","params":{"k":[1,5]}}],"explain":{"kid":["Before you choose, stop and think: how will the other person feel? If Tom wants a go with the ball, giving him a turn makes him smile. Keeping it all day makes him sad.","Tell the truth, even when you made a mistake. An accident is not naughty. If you hide it or say the cat did it, people find out and trust you less. If you tell, they can help you fix it.","Some things are yours, like your teddy and your lunch. Some things belong to someone else, like a friend's crayons. Ask first. A toy you find in the park is still someone's. Give it to a grown-up.","Fair means each person gets the same. Six strawberries for three children is two each. The right thing can be hard, like saying no to a friend. It is still right, and you feel good after."],"adult":["This topic sits in the primary Relationships Education guidance for England. It asks pupils to learn why honesty and truthfulness matter, to respect other people and their things, and to be fair in friendships. PSHE plans for Key Stage 1 add the difference between right and wrong, and how choices have consequences for us and for others.","Children of five to seven are building a conscience: a sense of right and wrong that works even when no adult is watching. Four skills carry it. Take the other person's view (how will Tom feel?). Own up after an accident. Respect property: a thing is not yours just because no one is there. See fair as equal shares. Praise the choice and the reason, not only the result.","Common mistakes at this age: judging an act by the size of the damage, not the intention, so ten cups broken by accident seem worse than one broken on purpose. Thinking a lie is fine if no one finds out. Believing finders keepers. Treating fair as whatever the finder or the oldest child wants. Be careful not to punish a child who owns up to an accident, or they learn to hide things.","In the lab your child picks kind or unkind choices and watches a face change. They play out telling the truth, hiding a broken cup and blaming the cat, while Mum's trust hearts rise or fall. Then they sort six things into ask first or mine to use, share six strawberries fairly, and take the harder right path three times."]},"familyTip":"When something goes wrong at home, say thank you first when your child owns up, then fix it together. Ask: how do you think they felt? and what would be fair?","keyIdea":"A right choice is honest, fair and kind, and it thinks about how other people will feel. Tell the truth, even after an accident: hiding it or blaming someone else breaks trust. Ask before you use things that belong to someone else. Share so each person gets the same. The right thing is sometimes the harder thing, and it is still right.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Right choice or wrong choice? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["👍 Right choice","👎 Wrong choice"],"items":[["Tell the truth about a spill",0],["Ask before you borrow",0],["Share the sweets the same",0],["Help a friend who fell",0],["Blame your brother",1],["Take a toy without asking",1],["Keep all the sweets",1],["Laugh when someone falls",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagright","caption":"Flagship lab: choose kindly and watch faces change, play out the truth about a broken cup, ask before you borrow, share fairly and take the harder right path.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_I9iSzpGRn5":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"What makes bullying different from a falling out?","choices":["It is on purpose, again and again, and hard to stop","Someone gets cross","It happens at school"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of the STOP check: Several Times On Purpose.","explain":"Bullying is on purpose, repeated, and the other person has more power, so it is hard to stop. Friends can get cross once and make up."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Every day Kim tells the others, \"Don't play with Ana.\" What kind of bullying is it?","choices":["Social bullying","Physical bullying","It is not bullying because no one is hit"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is Ana hurt in her body, or left out?","explain":"Leaving someone out on purpose, again and again, is social bullying. Bullying does not need hitting."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Leo is being bullied. A friend says, \"Don't tell, it will get worse.\" What should Leo do?","choices":["Tell a trusted grown-up","Keep it secret","Hit back"],"answer":0,"hint":"Who has the power to make it stop?","explain":"A trusted grown-up can stop it. Secrets let bullying go on, and hitting back can get Leo hurt or in trouble."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m4","prompt":"Max has {k} friends who join in. With Max, how many are on the bully's side?","answer":"k+1","hint":"Count Max as well as his friends.","explain":"{k} friends and Max make {=k+1}. A group against one child is a power imbalance, so it is hard for that child to stop it alone.","params":{"k":[1,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"You see Ella being pushed over every playtime. What does an upstander do?","choices":["Gets a grown-up and is kind to Ella","Watches and says nothing","Pushes the bully"],"answer":0,"hint":"An upstander helps in a safe way.","explain":"An upstander gets a grown-up and is kind to Ella. Watching does not help, and pushing could get someone hurt."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"Jo got a mean message on {n} days. Each day there were 2 messages. How many messages should Jo save to show a grown-up?","answer":"2*n","hint":"2 messages each day, for {n} days.","explain":"2 messages on each of {n} days is {=2*n} messages. Saving them all shows the grown-up it keeps happening.","params":{"n":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Ben kicks a ball and it hits Rosa by accident. He says sorry. Is it bullying?","choices":["No, it was an accident","Yes, Rosa got hurt","Yes, it was physical"],"answer":0,"hint":"Did Ben mean to do it? Did it keep happening?","explain":"It was not on purpose, and it happened once. Getting hurt by accident is not bullying."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Raj told his teacher he is being bullied, but it has not stopped. What now?","choices":["Tell again, or tell another grown-up","Give up, telling does not work","Sort it out alone"],"answer":0,"hint":"Telling once is not always enough.","explain":"Keep telling until it stops. Raj can tell his teacher again, or tell a parent or another grown-up at school."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Which one is bullying?","choices":["Ola calls Finn a mean name every day","Ola and Finn argue once about a game","Ola trips over Finn's bag by accident"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look for something done on purpose, again and again.","explain":"Calling Finn a name every day is on purpose and repeated. That is verbal bullying. An argument once or an accident is not bullying."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"Max hid Ava's bag on {n} days this week. How many times did it happen?","answer":"n","hint":"Count one for each day he hid the bag.","explain":"It happened {n} times. Again and again, on purpose, is part of what makes it bullying.","params":{"n":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Mean messages about Jade keep coming in a group chat. What kind of bullying is it?","choices":["Cyberbullying","Physical bullying","It is not bullying"],"answer":0,"hint":"Where are the messages being sent?","explain":"Hurtful messages sent again and again online are cyberbullying. Jade should save them and show a trusted grown-up."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"{n} children see Sam being pushed. {k} of them go and get a teacher. How many are only watching?","answer":"n-k","hint":"Take away the children who went to get help.","explain":"{n} take away {k} is {=n-k}. Those {=n-k} are bystanders. They could help too, by getting a grown-up or being kind to Sam.","params":{"n":[4,8],"k":[1,3]}}],"explain":{"kid":["Friends fall out sometimes. They shout, then say sorry and play again. That is not bullying. Bullying is different. Someone hurts you on purpose. They do it again and again. And it is hard for you to make them stop.","Why is it hard to stop? The bully may be older or bigger. They may have a gang of friends. They may know a secret. That is called power.","There are four kinds. Physical is pushing or breaking your things. Verbal is mean names or threats. Social is leaving you out or telling lies about you. Cyber is mean messages or posts online.","Bullying is never your fault. Tell a grown-up you trust. Save mean messages to show them. If it does not stop, tell again. If you see it, be an upstander. Do not laugh along. Be kind to the person, and get a grown-up."],"adult":["In England, Relationships Education is taught in every primary school. It covers the different types of bullying, including cyberbullying, and the harm it does. It also covers what bystanders should do (mainly tell an adult) and how to get help. The Anti-Bullying Alliance defines bullying as the repeated, intentional hurting of one person or group by another, where there is an imbalance of power. StopBullying.gov in the US uses the same three parts: unwanted aggressive behaviour, repetition, and a real or perceived power imbalance.","Key terms for this age: repeated, on purpose (intentional), power imbalance, physical, verbal, social or relational bullying (leaving out, rumours), cyberbullying, bystander, upstander, trusted adult and report. Many schools teach the STOP check: Several Times On Purpose. Power can come from size or age, from numbers, from being popular, or from knowing something private.","Common mistakes: calling every argument bullying, so the word loses its weight. Or the opposite: deciding that name-calling or leaving someone out is not real bullying because nobody was hit. Children often think telling makes it worse. They may hit back, reply to cruel messages, or delete them so there is no proof. A common adult slip is to say \"just ignore it\" or \"sort it out yourselves\" when the child faces a power gap they cannot fix alone.","In the lab your child marks the days a story happened on a week strip, to tell a falling out from a pattern. They sort accidents from things done on purpose. They load a see-saw with the bully's power, then level it with helpers. They sort examples into the four kinds, and sort actions into ones that help and ones that make it worse."]},"familyTip":"Ask, \"What would you do if someone kept being mean to a friend at school?\" and agree together who their trusted grown-ups are, at school and at home. If your child tells you about bullying, listen calmly, thank them for telling, write down what happened and when, and speak to the school.","keyIdea":"Bullying is hurting someone on purpose, again and again, when it is hard for them to stop it because the other person or group has more power. It can be physical (hitting, breaking things), verbal (names, threats), social (leaving someone out, spreading rumours) or cyberbullying (online). A one-off falling out or an accident is not bullying. Bullying is never the fault of the person it happens to. If it happens to you or you see it, tell a trusted grown-up, save any messages, and keep telling until it stops.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Bullying or a falling out? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Bullying","Not bullying"],"items":[["Kit hides Mo's lunch every day and laughs",0],["Two friends argue once, then make up",1],["A gang tells Ana she can't play, all week",0],["Ben bumps Jo by accident in the line",1],["Mean posts about Leo, again and again",0],["Sam and Ella both want the swing, once",1],["An older boy takes Raj's ball every playtime",0],["A friend says sorry after a game gets rough",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagbully","caption":"Flagship lab: falling out or bullying on a week strip, on purpose or accident, a power see-saw, the four kinds, and what helps.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_yCmYV9ruQu":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Ivy films Zak being mean to Mia and shows her friends. What is Ivy doing?","choices":["Joining in","Being an upstander","Nothing wrong"],"answer":0,"hint":"Does filming help Mia, or give Zak an audience?","explain":"Filming and sharing it gives Zak an audience and hurts Mia more. That is joining in."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Which of these is a safe upstander move?","choices":["Ask the child to come and play with you","Shout mean words back","Hit the unkind child"],"answer":0,"hint":"A safe move helps the hurt child without making it bigger.","explain":"Standing with the child helps them and keeps you safe. Being mean back or hitting makes it worse."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Why can a big crowd mean that nobody helps?","choices":["Each one waits for someone else","Big crowds are always kind","Nobody in a crowd can see"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about what each child in the crowd is thinking.","explain":"Each one thinks someone else will help, so nobody does. Be the first, and others will join."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m4","prompt":"{n} children watch someone being teased. {k} of them laugh. How many are only watching, not laughing?","answer":"n-k","hint":"Take the laughing children away from all the children watching.","explain":"{n} take away {k} is {=n-k} bystanders. They could still choose to help.","params":{"n":[4,8],"k":[1,3]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Mia looked sad at lunch, but it is over now. What can an upstander do later?","choices":["Ask Mia if she is OK","Forget about it","Tell everyone what happened"],"answer":0,"hint":"How can you show Mia that someone cares?","explain":"Checking in later is an upstander move. It shows Mia she is not on her own."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"You tell a teacher that a child is bullied every day. This is…","choices":["Keeping the child safe","Telling tales","Being mean"],"answer":0,"hint":"Telling tales is to get someone into trouble. Why are you telling?","explain":"You are telling to keep a child safe, so it is the right thing to do, not telling tales."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Raj sees Zak being unkind and walks past without a word. What is Raj?","choices":["A bystander","An upstander","The one being unkind"],"answer":0,"hint":"Did Raj help, join in, or do nothing?","explain":"Raj saw it and did nothing, so Raj is a bystander. He could choose a safe way to help."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m8","prompt":"Kai stands with Mia. Then {k} friends come and stand with them. How many children stand with Mia now, not counting Mia?","answer":"k+1","hint":"Count Kai, then add the friends who came.","explain":"Kai plus {k} friends is {=k+1} children standing with Mia. A group makes the unkind child stop.","params":{"k":[1,5]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Bystanders and upstanders: Kai sees Zak take Mia's ball. Kai says, 'Give it back, that is not kind.' What is Kai?","choices":["An upstander","A bystander","Joining in"],"answer":0,"hint":"Did Kai help Mia, watch, or join in with Zak?","explain":"Kai spoke up to help Mia in a safe way, so Kai is an upstander."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"{n} children watch Zak tease Mia, and they all laugh. Then {k} of them stop laughing and walk off with Mia. How many are still laughing?","answer":"n-k","hint":"Take the children who stopped away from all the children who laughed.","explain":"{n} take away {k} is {=n-k}. Each child who stops laughing makes Zak's audience smaller.","params":{"n":[5,9],"k":[1,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Two children are pushing hard and someone could get hurt. What should an upstander do?","choices":["Get a grown-up fast","Push back to stop them","Watch to see what happens"],"answer":0,"hint":"When someone could get hurt, what keeps everyone safe?","explain":"Pushing back could get you hurt, and watching lets it go on. Get a grown-up fast."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"Ada is the first to help a child who is left out. Then {k} more children join her. How many helpers are there now?","answer":"k+1","hint":"Count Ada, then count on the children who joined her.","explain":"Ada plus {k} more makes {=k+1} helpers. Once one child goes first, others follow.","params":{"k":[1,6]}}],"explain":{"kid":["Zak is mean to Mia. Other kids see it. Each one has a choice. A kid who joins in laughs or cheers. A bystander just looks on. An upstander helps Mia.","Doing nothing is a choice too. If kids laugh, Zak has a crowd. If no one laughs, his show is over. So do not laugh. Walk off with Mia.","Lots of kids may see it. Each one thinks: someone else will help. Then no one does. Be the first one. Then the rest will join you.","Help in a safe way. Say: Stop, that is not kind. Ask Mia to play. If a kid could get hurt, get a grown-up fast. To tell to keep a kid safe is not to tell tales."],"adult":["This topic covers the bystander role in relationships education. In England, the statutory guidance for primary schools says pupils should learn about the responsibilities of bystanders, mainly reporting bullying to an adult, and how to get help. The IB PYP personal, social and physical education strand asks children to show care and stand up for others. At 7 to 9 the aim is to name the roles, choose a safe action and feel able to use it.","Key terms: a passive bystander watches and does nothing. Children who laugh, cheer or film are joining in, because they give the unkind child an audience. An upstander speaks up, stands with the target, tells an adult or checks in later. In Canadian playground studies, peers were there in most bullying episodes. When one peer stepped in, the bullying often stopped within seconds. The bystander effect, or diffusion of responsibility, is why a big crowd can mean less help: each person waits for someone else.","Common mistakes: thinking that doing nothing is neutral; thinking that laughing along is harmless; teaching children to step in with their hands, or to be mean back, which puts them at risk; and calling reporting telling tales. Be careful with the rule. Telling to keep someone safe is always right. Telling tales is telling to get someone into trouble. Safety comes first, so when someone could get hurt, the job is to fetch an adult fast.","In the lab your child sorts what each child does, stops a laughing crowd and watches the audience bar fall, and goes first so the others follow. Then they pick a safe move for scenes of low and high risk, and sort telling from telling tales."]},"familyTip":"Agree a family upstander plan: three safe moves your child can use, such as 'Stop, that's not kind', 'Come and play with us' and telling an adult. Act them out with toys, then ask after school: did anyone need an upstander today?","keyIdea":"When someone is being unkind, every child who sees it makes a choice. Joining in means laughing, cheering or filming. A bystander watches and does nothing. An upstander helps in a safe way: says stop, stands with the hurt child, tells a grown-up or checks in later. If someone could get hurt, get a grown-up fast. Telling to keep someone safe is never telling tales.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Who is an upstander? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Upstander","Bystander","Joins in"],"items":[["Says: Stop, that is not kind",0],["Watches and says nothing",1],["Laughs along",2],["Tells a teacher",0],["Films it on a phone",2],["Looks away and walks past",1],["Asks the hurt child to play",0]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagupst","caption":"Flagship lab: sort what each child does, stop a laughing crowd, be the first helper, pick a safe move for the risk, and sort telling from telling tales.","opts":{"step":0}}}});
